<a id=3D"#TTID:" 511299=3D"" name=3D"TTID:511299"></a>
<b><font color=3D"#3399FF">KPR#:511299&nbsp;&nbsp;Product:OpenLAB - CDS2&nb=
sp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2.5</font></b><br>
<p><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"3">Keyword:&nbsp;</font></p>
<p><b><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"3">One-line Description:</font>=
</b><br>
</p><pre><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"2">Scaling factor for area c=
onversion of imported EZChrom data</font></pre><p></p>
<p><b><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"3">Problem:</font></b><br>
</p><pre><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"2">An analysis done by CDS 2=
.x results in different peak areas and heights compared to EZChrom Elite an=
d OpenLAB CDS EZChrom edition. While calibrated results, Area% and Height% =
are not affected, absolute peak areas and heights differ by detector depend=
ent scaling factors in case you analyze the same sample with the same metho=
d in both CDSs.=20
Also, when you import EZChrom data into CDS 2.x, the EZChrom units for peak=
 area and peak height are maintained and differ from the results from sampl=
es being analyzed directly by CDS 2.x.  </font></pre><p></p>
<p><b><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"3">Temporary Solution:</font></=
b><br>
</p><pre><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"2">n/a</font></pre><p></p>
<p><b><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"3">Fix Information:</font></b><=
br>
</p><pre><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"2">Using the 2.x Intelligent=
 Reporting or Custom Calculation, a detector dependent scaling factor can b=
e applied in order to adjust the CDS 2.x results for better comparison, for=
 example in the context of method validation.=20

The scaling factor only applies to the results.  It does not change raw dat=
a.=20

Following the detailed calculation and a list of detector dependent scaling=
 factors:

Scaled areas/heights with detector specific units can be obtained by multip=
lying the unit-less EZChrom =E2=80=9Ccounts=E2=80=9D by the matching detect=
or specific scaling factor.=20
Peak Height [CDS 2.x] =3D Peak Height [EZChrom] * Scaling factor
Peak Area [CDS 2.x] =3D Peak Area [EZChrom] * Scaling factor
Likewise, unscaled areas/heights, i.e. detector counts, can be obtained by =
dividing the CDS 2.x data by the scaling factor.=20
Peak Height [EZChrom] =3D Peak Height [CDS 2.x] / Scaling factor
Peak Area [EZChrom] =3D Peak Area [CDS 2.x] / Scaling factor

In order to obtain CDS 2.x equivalent values for GC signals in the appropri=
ate scale for each detector, the reported value in EZChrom must be divided =
by 7680 for all detector types. This is based on the GC Least Significant V=
alue (1/7680), i.e. the scaling factor to put into the formulas is 1/7680.=
=20

For Agilent LC detector signals the scaling factors used to transform Peak =
Area and Height from counts to detector units are as follows:
VWD Detector Models - G1314A\G1314B\G1314C\G1314D\G1314E\G1314F\G7114A\G711=
4B
Scaling Factor =3D 0.000059604644775390625 mAU (=3D1000/2^24)
DAD\MWD Detector Models - G1315A\G1315B\G1315C\G1315D\G1365A\G1365B\G1365C\=
G1365D\G7115A
Scaling Factor =3D 0.000476837158203125 mAU (=3D1000/2^21)
DAD Detector Models - G4212A\G4212B\G7117A\G7117B\G7117C
Scaling Factor =3D 0.000007450580596923828125 mAU (=3D1000/2^27)
FLD Detector Models - G1321A\G1321B\G1321C
Scaling Factor - 0.0000059604644775390625 LU (=3D100/2^24)
RID Detector Models G1362A\G7162A\G7162B
Scaling Factor =3D 100 nRIU=20
</font></pre><p></p>




