</p><pre><font face=3D"arial,sanserif" size=3D"2">When building a linear ca=
libration curve with 2 points including (0,0), we expect an equation like y=
=3Dax. But in some cases, the originate at the origin could be different fr=
om zero (for example: y=3Dax+5e-17). The equation (y=3Dax+b) is always comp=
uted when a Linear calibration curve is selected. Real values (which have a=
 precision of 15 digits) are used for the computations (which leads to valu=
es with a higher precision).=20
This difference in precision could lead to a =E2=80=9Cb=E2=80=9D value diff=
erent from 0 (between 1e-15 and 1e-17) as expected. This value is below the=
 precision of real values.
</font></pre><p></p>
